{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NyADXFVwHTzAYZmp5qbQvC","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"Based on my search of the context filesystem, I found the answer to your question about the cleanup policy configuration for the audit remote log metadata topic.\\\\n\\\\n## Answer\\\\n\\\\nThe **final decided cleanup policy configuration should use 'compact'** (not 'compact,delete' and not just 'delete').\\\\n\\\\n### Key Decision Points (from the email thread):\\\\n\\\\n1. **Kamal's initial proposal** (Jan 14-15): Suggested keeping cleanup policy as \\\\\\\"delete\\\\\\\" initially, with users later migrating to \\\\\\\"[compact, delete]\\\\\\\" after their retention periods expired.\\\\n\\\\n2. **Lijun Tong's objection** (Jan 15-16): Explicitly rejected keeping \\\\\\\"delete\\\\\\\" in the final cleanup policy:\\\\n   > \\\\\\\"I don't think it's a good idea to keep delete in the final cleanup policy for the topic `__remote_log_metadata`, as this still requires the user to keep track of the max retention hours of topics that have remote storage enabled, and it's operational burden. It's also hard to reason about what will happen if the user configures the wrong retention.ms.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n3. **Community consensus** (Jan 16-20): Jian and Kamal agreed with this position. The final decision settled on using only **'compact'** as the cleanup policy to eliminate the operational burden and reduce the risk of misconfiguration.\\\\n\\\\n### Sources:\\\\n- `_intake/2026/01/15/mail-cf97670bbdc327e681e9529b-9abceaea.md` (Lijun's rejection of compact,delete)\\\\n- `_intake/2026/01/18/mail-8798ead7dd94fed1e7820b5c-422fb6a7.md` (Kamal and Jian's agreement)\\\\n- `_intake/2026/01/20/mail-34fc284fbaae3665c418057e-b3176fec.md` (Final confirmation in follow-up)\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"_intake/2026/01/08/mail-b489a1b13ef8985cb665d11b-c46c123a.md\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/01/15/mail-cf97670bbdc327e681e95